## Idempotency In API Design

## Definition

```
f(f(x)) = f(x)

Calling an operation once has the same observable effect
as calling it N times with the same input.
```

## HTTP Methods and Idempotency

```
Method    Idempotent?   Safe?   Notes
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GET       ✅ Yes        ✅ Yes  Read-only, no side effects
HEAD      ✅ Yes        ✅ Yes  Same as GET, no body
OPTIONS   ✅ Yes        ✅ Yes  No state change
PUT       ✅ Yes        ❌ No   Replace same resource same way
DELETE    ✅ Yes        ❌ No   Deleting twice = resource gone
POST      ❌ No         ❌ No   Creates new resource each time
PATCH     ⚠️  Depends  ❌ No   { name: 'X' } is idempotent;
                                { increment: 1 } is not
```

## Idempotency Keys For POST

```typescript
// Client sends a UUID with each request
fetch('/api/orders', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'Idempotency-Key': crypto.randomUUID(), // generated once, stored by client
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ productId: 'abc', quantity: 1 }),
});

// Server (simplified)
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const key = req.headers.get('Idempotency-Key');
  if (!key) return Response.json({ error: 'Missing Idempotency-Key' }, { status: 400 });

  // Check cache
  const cached = await redis.get(`idem:${key}`);
  if (cached) return Response.json(JSON.parse(cached)); // return previous result

  // Process the order
  const order = await createOrder(req);

  // Cache result (expire after 24h)
  await redis.setex(`idem:${key}`, 86400, JSON.stringify(order));

  return Response.json(order, { status: 201 });
}
```

## Who Uses This

```
Stripe    → Idempotency-Key on all payment requests
Twilio    → Idempotency keys on message sends
PayPal    → PayPal-Request-Id header
```

## Consistent API Error Handling

## Error Response Shape

```typescript
// RFC 7807 Problem Details (standardised)
interface ApiError {
  type: string;       // URI identifying error type
  title: string;      // human-readable summary
  status: number;     // HTTP status code
  detail?: string;    // specific instance description
  errors?: { field: string; message: string }[]; // validation errors
}

// Example validation error
{
  "type": "https://api.example.com/errors/validation",
  "title": "Validation Error",
  "status": 422,
  "detail": "The request body contains invalid fields",
  "errors": [
    { "field": "email", "message": "Must be a valid email address" },
    { "field": "age", "message": "Must be a positive integer" }
  ]
}
```

## Centralised Error Handler (Next.js)

```typescript
// lib/api-error.ts
export class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(
    public status: number,
    public code: string,
    message: string,
    public details?: unknown,
  ) {
    super(message);
  }
}

export function handleApiError(err: unknown): Response {
  if (err instanceof ApiError) {
    return Response.json(
      { error: { code: err.code, message: err.message, details: err.details } },
      { status: err.status },
    );
  }
  // Unexpected errors — log but don't expose internals
  console.error('[API Error]', err);
  return Response.json(
    { error: { code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR', message: 'An unexpected error occurred' } },
    { status: 500 },
  );
}

// In a route handler:
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  try {
    // ...
  } catch (err) {
    return handleApiError(err);
  }
}
```

## Machine-Readable Error Codes

```
ALWAYS include a string code alongside the HTTP status.
Reason: HTTP status is coarse. Your code is precise.

400 Bad Request can mean:
  MISSING_REQUIRED_FIELD
  INVALID_DATE_FORMAT
  UNKNOWN_ENUM_VALUE

This lets clients handle specific errors differently
without string-matching error messages.
```

## The N+1 Problem

## How It Happens

```typescript
// ❌ N+1 — 1 query for posts + N queries for authors
const posts = await db.query('SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT 20'); // 1 query
const result = await Promise.all(
  posts.map(async (post) => {
    const author = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [post.authorId]); // N queries
    return { ...post, author: author[0] };
  }),
);
// = 21 queries 😱
```

## Fix 1: JOIN in One Query

```typescript
// ✅ 1 query total
const posts = await db.query(`
  SELECT p.*, u.name AS author_name, u.avatar AS author_avatar
  FROM posts p
  JOIN users u ON u.id = p.author_id
  LIMIT 20
`);
```

## Fix 2: Eager Loading (Prisma)

```typescript
// ✅ Prisma generates an efficient JOIN
const posts = await prisma.post.findMany({
  take: 20,
  include: { author: { select: { name: true, avatar: true } } },
});
```

## Fix 3: DataLoader (GraphQL)

```typescript
import DataLoader from 'dataloader';

const userLoader = new DataLoader(async (ids: readonly string[]) => {
  const users = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ANY($1)', [ids]);
  return ids.map(id => users.find(u => u.id === id));
});

// Now resolving 20 post authors = 1 batched query instead of 20
const author = await userLoader.load(post.authorId);
```

## Fix 4: Collect IDs, Batch Fetch

```typescript
// ✅ 2 queries regardless of N
const posts = await db.query('SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT 20');
const authorIds = [...new Set(posts.map(p => p.authorId))];
const authors = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ANY($1)', [authorIds]);
const authorMap = Object.fromEntries(authors.map(a => [a.id, a]));
const result = posts.map(p => ({ ...p, author: authorMap[p.authorId] }));
```

## Webhooks vs Polling

## Polling

```
Client → 'Anything new?' → Server: 'No'
Client → 'Anything new?' → Server: 'No'
Client → 'Anything new?' → Server: 'No'
Client → 'Anything new?' → Server: 'YES! Here's the data'

✅ Simple — no public URL needed
✅ Works behind firewalls
❌ Wasteful — most calls get 'no'
❌ Latency = polling interval (up to 5–60 seconds)
❌ Doesn't scale (100k clients × 12 req/min = 1.2M req/min)
```

## Webhooks

```
Provider → POST /webhooks/stripe → Your server
  (only when something happens)

✅ Real-time — triggered instantly
✅ Efficient — no wasted calls
❌ Requires public HTTPS endpoint
❌ Must handle retries (providers retry on 4xx/5xx)
❌ Must verify signatures
❌ Must be idempotent
```

## Secure Webhook Handler

```typescript
import { createHmac } from 'crypto';

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const payload = await req.text();
  const signature = req.headers.get('Stripe-Signature')!;
  const secret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET!;

  // 1. Verify signature
  const hmac = createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(payload)
    .digest('hex');
  if (hmac !== signature.split('=')[1]) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Invalid signature' }, { status: 401 });
  }

  const event = JSON.parse(payload);

  // 2. Idempotency — skip if already processed
  const processed = await redis.get(`webhook:${event.id}`);
  if (processed) return Response.json({ ok: true }); // 200 to stop retries

  // 3. Handle event
  if (event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded') {
    await fulfillOrder(event.data.object);
  }

  // 4. Mark as processed
  await redis.setex(`webhook:${event.id}`, 86400, '1');
  return Response.json({ ok: true });
}
```

## API Caching Layers

## Layer 1: HTTP Cache Headers

```typescript
// Public cacheable endpoint (product list)
export async function GET() {
  const products = await getProducts();
  return Response.json(products, {
    headers: {
      'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=300',
      // CDN serves stale for up to 5 min while revalidating in background
    },
  });
}

// Private (user-specific) — never cache at CDN
export async function GET() {
  const cart = await getUserCart(userId);
  return Response.json(cart, {
    headers: { 'Cache-Control': 'private, max-age=0' },
  });
}
```

## Layer 2: ETags / Conditional Requests

```typescript
import { createHash } from 'crypto';

export async function GET(req: Request) {
  const data = await getResource();
  const etag = createHash('md5').update(JSON.stringify(data)).digest('hex');

  if (req.headers.get('If-None-Match') === etag) {
    return new Response(null, { status: 304 }); // Not Modified — no body
  }

  return Response.json(data, { headers: { ETag: etag } });
}
```

## Layer 3: Server-Side Cache (Redis)

```typescript
export async function GET(req: Request, { params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const cacheKey = `product:${params.id}`;
  const cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (cached) return Response.json(JSON.parse(cached));

  const product = await db.getProduct(params.id);
  await redis.setex(cacheKey, 300, JSON.stringify(product)); // 5-min TTL
  return Response.json(product);
}
```

## Cache Invalidation Strategies

```
TTL-based   → Expire after N seconds (simple, eventual consistency)
Event-based → Invalidate on write: DELETE cache when product updated
Tag-based   → Cloudflare Cache Tags: purge all 'product-123' tagged responses
```

## High-Availability API Design

## The Four Nines

```
99%    → 3.65 days/year downtime
99.9%  → 8.76 hours/year downtime  (typical SLA target)
99.99% → 52.6 minutes/year downtime
99.999%→ 5.26 minutes/year downtime (hard to achieve)
```

## Redundancy

```
               [Load Balancer] (multiple — avoid single point of failure)
              /        |        \
          API-1       API-2     API-3  (multiple instances, multiple AZs)
              \        |        /
          [Primary DB] ─── [Read Replica x2]
                |                  |
            [Redis Cluster]   [Redis Replica]
```

## Circuit Breaker Pattern

```typescript
class CircuitBreaker {
  private failures = 0;
  private state: 'closed' | 'open' | 'half-open' = 'closed';
  private nextAttempt = Date.now();

  async call<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, fallback: () => T): Promise<T> {
    if (this.state === 'open') {
      if (Date.now() < this.nextAttempt) return fallback();
      this.state = 'half-open';
    }
    try {
      const result = await fn();
      this.failures = 0;
      this.state = 'closed';
      return result;
    } catch (err) {
      this.failures++;
      if (this.failures >= 5) {
        this.state = 'open';
        this.nextAttempt = Date.now() + 30_000; // try again in 30s
      }
      return fallback();
    }
  }
}

// Usage: when recommendations service is down, return empty array
const recs = await breaker.call(
  () => fetchRecommendations(userId),
  () => [],
);
```

## Retry With Exponential Backoff + Jitter

```typescript
async function withRetry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, maxRetries = 3): Promise<T> {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === maxRetries) throw err;
      const base = Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attempt, 30_000);
      const jitter = Math.random() * 1000;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, base + jitter));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('unreachable');
}
```
